we begin this morning with breaking news  overnight and a tragic end to an  hours-long hostage situation at the  nation's largest veterans retirement  home authorities in Napa County  California say the suspect an Army  veteran and three women were found dead  in a room at the facility in Yountville  north of San Francisco the victims  identified as Christine lobe ER dr.  Jennifer Golic and dr. Jennifer Gonzalez  all worked for a nonprofit that helps  veterans of the wars in Iraq and  Afghanistan Carter Evans is in  Yountville Carter good morning good  morning the suspect is identified as  Albert Wong he's a decorated former  infantryman now investigators haven't  released a motive for the attack right  now but reports indicate that Wong had  recently been kicked out of the  treatment program here by one of the  victims napa county sheriff's deputies  were called to the veteran's retirement  home of yan field just after 10:00 a.m.  on Friday investigators say the suspect  albert wong made his way into an  employee party then brandishing an  assault rifle took three women hostage  and started shooting a deputy exchanged  gunfire with the suspect but police had  no more contact with him as he took the  three hostages into a room in one of the  buildings on the sprawling campus he  walked in with a rifle so I think people  had a pretty clear understanding about  what was gonna what was going on there's  obviously no firearms allowed in that  building the home state of lockdown for  nearly eight hours but after failing to  establish contact with wong police made  their move law enforcement personnel  made entry into the room  where we felt the hostages were being  held by the suspect and unfortunately  made the discovery of three deceased  females and one deceased male suspect  military records show Wang was awarded  five medals and a badge for marksmanship  while serving in the Army he spent one  year in Afghanistan during his three  years of active service the three  victims were committed to serving  veterans of Iraq and Afghanistan they  worked for the pathway home a program  that treats vets for post-traumatic  stress disorder in a statement pathway  home said these brave women were  accomplished professionals who dedicated  their careers to serving our nation's  veterans now police are crediting the  first deputy on the scene was stopping  wang from finding more victims there are  security guards here but the association  that represents those guards says  they're not allowed to carry weapons  Anthony Carter Evans in Yountville  California Thank You Carter
 

